wins": 0.966}], "watch": ["Hinds County (Jackson): about 9,000 regular ballots still expected, where Colom leads the counted vote by 61 points. If Hinds turns out to be nearly finished, Colom's chance falls to about 68%.", "Rankin County: about 6,000 still expected, where Hyde-Smith leads by 26 points. It is the biggest offset to Hinds.", "Affidavit (provisional) ballots: 0 of 82 counties have reported official counts so far; the rest are estimated.", "Voters who cast an affidavit ballot for lack of photo ID have five business days (to about Nov. 10) to show ID at the circuit clerk's office. Mail ballots postmarked by Nov. 3 count if they arrive by then too.", "Mississippi has no automatic recount and no runoff in this race. A recount needs a court order."], "changed": {"since": "5:30 PM Nov 4", "head": "Colom's chance went from 96% to 97%; most likely final margin Colom +5,400 to Colom +7,200.", "items": ["No new votes were counted.", "AP changed its estimate of ballots left to count in 2 counties (46,650 to 47,050 statewide). Biggest effects: Clay, 500 more (Colom +34 pts there); Walthall, 100 fewer (Hyde-Smith +8 pts there). Together these shift the expected outcome about 180 votes toward Colom.", "The model was updated to rely more on data and less on assumptions: it now uses each county's record on affidavit ballots from the last three federal elections (about 7 in 10 accepted, not the half it assumed before), the Secretary of State's current count of outstanding mail ballots, and how the batches counted so far actually compared with expectations. On its own this left Colom's chance about the same but widened the range of possible final margins.", "Hinds County has not posted new numbers yet."]}, "summary": "Colom is favored, but the race is not over. Colom leads by 1,138 votes with about 47,000 ballots left to count. The biggest batch is in Hinds County (about 9,000), where Colom is winning about 4 in 5 votes.", "page_version": "41af7da8"}